our planet is home to a puzzle involving  chiral molecules these molecules come in  two varieties both made up of the same  atoms and bonds and even though they are  mirror images they don't exactly match  it's like trying to put a right-handed  glove onto your left hand  life on earth is made up of groups of  chiral molecules that share just one  handedness this is called homo chirality  for example the amino acids that make up  the proteins and our bodies are all  left-handed without any bias one way or  the other chemical reactions will make  equal amounts of left and right-handed  molecules but in order to make a group  of homo chiral molecules the bias must  already be there to begin with so the  source of the homo chirality in  biological systems the see of this  chiral bias is one of the deepest  mysteries in chemistry researchers think  the solution might lie in the balance of  chiral molecules in gas clouds in the  interstellar medium that's the matter  that exists between the stars in our  galaxy scientists think that at one  point the balance must have tipped in  favor of one handedness over another but  a chiral molecule has never been  observed in space until now recently  scientists aimed radio telescopes deep  near the center of our Milky Way galaxy  at an interstellar cloud Sagittarius b2  sure enough they detected radio waves  that bear the fingerprints of the chiral  molecule propylene oxide this discovery  is the first step in seeing if chirality  in space can explain homo chirality on  since it's thought that interstellar  clouds contained the raw ingredients for  the formation of our solar system  studying the chiral molecules inside  them may even shed light on the origins  of life itself the next step is for  scientists to determine if the propylene  oxide molecules they've detected are  mostly righties or lefties
 

